    "prose": "Another surge struck the fixed grate, and water boiled below the dry ledge. The hatchling shrank against the wall, trembling.\n\nEkin dragged her gaze away from the flooding lower room. “I frightened it. I chased the compass, and now it’s trapped.” She faced Duru, forcing the words out while Duru watched her. “This is my fault, so I have to get it back to its mother.”\n\nThunder shook the lighthouse. The hatchling squeaked.\n\nEkin pulled off her jacket. “Duru, will you help me rescue it?”\n\nDuru met her eyes and nodded. “Yes. Tell me what you need.”\n\nEkin crouched on the dry stair and held her jacket open toward the ledge. Duru watched as she lowered it within the hatchling’s reach.\n\n“Come on,” Ekin murmured. “I won’t chase you.”\n\nThe little dragon sniffed the jacket. As water slapped near the ledge, it scrambled onto the cloth. Ekin folded the sides gently around its shivering body and drew it close.\n\nThe hatchling climbed from the lower ledge onto the lighthouse stair’s dry section.\n\nIts claws shifted inside the jacket, and Ekin’s grip slipped. “Take it!”\n\nDuru caught the bundled hatchling against her chest, steadying the handoff before it could tumble. Ekin saw her secure it and gave one sharp, grateful nod. “Good catch.”\n\nDuru climbed the narrow internal stair and entered the upper room.\n\nHeld safely in Duru’s arms, the hatchling passed into the upper room.\n\nEkin climbed the narrow internal stair and entered the upper room behind them.\n\nThe mother dragon waited there, unable to squeeze down the tight turn. She bent toward the bundle as Duru lowered it. The hatchling wriggled free of Ekin’s jacket and pressed against its mother.\n\nEkin and Duru stood together in the dry upper room, breathing hard while the storm battered the lighthouse.\n\nEkin took back her damp jacket. “I couldn’t have done that alone.”\n\n“I know,” Duru said, without teasing.\n\nEkin glanced at her. They were still strangers—but not quite as much as before.",
